  "articleBody": "When you're thinking about making your bedroom more accessible, it's easy to fall into some common traps that actually make things harder. One big mistake is leaving area rugs in place; they're huge trip hazards! Another is not clearing pathways completely – remember, you need a full 36 inches for clear routes. Don't overlook placing controls too high either; light switches and thermostat controls should be within easy reach. And be super careful about bed heights and furniture layouts that make transfers tough. If your bed coverings drag on the floor, that's another fall risk! Many folks also forget about proper lighting, especially at night, which can lead to stumbles. To avoid these issues, always prioritize clear pathways, ensure all frequently used items are within easy reach, and choose a bed height that allows for safe transfers. If you're planning any structural changes, like widening a doorway, or if you need a personalized assessment, a professional contractor or occupational therapist can guide you through the process, helping you sidestep these common blunders and create a truly accessible space.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}